// CONTROL w7b -- the OTHER direction of w7, added 2026-08-14.
// MEASURED: this already PASSES on the buggy compiler (Shape is registered first,
// so the name-only lookup happens to return Shape=1). It is therefore a CONTROL:
// a fix that merely reorders the table would break it -- never trade one
// miscompile for another. Shape::Mark is 1 in Shape; Color::Mark is 2 in Color.
// EXPECT: exit 1 (NOT 2).
// 2026-10-06 (#2800): Quad was `Quad{w: i64}` and the arm below `Shape::Quad{w}`.
// E264 now refuses every payload pattern (KL-20: an enum value is a bare
// discriminant, so a payload binding read garbage), which made this control a
// COMPILE-FAIL. The payload was incidental to what w7b measures -- variant
// lookup by enum, not by name -- so Quad is payload-free here. The
// discriminants are unchanged (Quad=0, Mark=1 in Shape; Mark=2 in Color) and
// the 99 arm still catches a lookup that sends Shape::Mark to Quad.
enum Shape { Quad, Mark }
enum Color { Red, Green, Mark }
fn main() -> i64 {
    let s: Shape = Shape::Mark
    match s {
        Shape::Quad => 99,
        Shape::Mark => 1,
        _ => 0,
    }
}
